The document does not claim that the seat belongs to Wiosna; the constitution
does not make deputies delegates of a party. It says something narrower: a
candidate who changes camps must face their voters, disclose every negotiation
and seek a place on the new party's list at the next election without a
protected transfer.

[? if left_dominant_current = "spring": Wiosna accepts the first signature
because the breach was its own. The pledge steadies the organisation and makes
every other deputy wonder which private conversation will next require a
public explanation.?]
[? if left_dominant_current = "razem": Razem gets the common ethical rule it
wanted without acquiring a legal power to recall MPs. Wiosna signs, then asks
why a rival had to draft its answer.?]
[? if left_dominant_current = "barons": The SLD executive turns an ethical
pledge into a whip's instrument within hours. Discipline improves on paper and
every smaller current starts keeping its own copy of the candidate list.?]
[? if left_dominant_current = "members": The member executive makes the
pledge reciprocal: leaders who change the programme must explain themselves as
fully as deputies who change the club.?]
[? if left_dominant_current != "spring" and left_dominant_current != "razem"
and left_dominant_current != "barons" and left_dominant_current != "members":
The federal parties sign the same words under separate logos. The standard is
common even where the organisations are not.?]

The pledge cannot return Pawłowska's vote. It makes the next departure harder
to describe as a private act with no debt to the list that made it possible.

