= A crowd that stays a crowd, or becomes something

Our people do not take the stage. They take the pavement: stewards, legal
observers, sign-up sheets, three thousand names and a list of who in which city
is prepared to do this again. The speeches belong to whoever booked the sound
system.

Two years from now, several of those names are running local campaigns. KO's
organisers, who assumed the square was theirs by default, notice which party
spent the evening collecting telephone numbers instead of applause.

