= October 2022 — The state discovers it has no coal counter

Roughly sixty per cent of Polish houses are heated by solid fuel and most of
them burn coal. The government banned Russian imports in April, which was
right, and did not replace the volume, which was not a decision anybody
admits making. By October the yards are empty, the price has roughly tripled,
and people are sleeping in cars in queues that form the night before a
delivery.

The answer arrives as a statute at the end of the month: communes are to buy
coal from state importers at a capped price and sell it on to residents. Every
mayor in the country is now a fuel merchant, with no warehouse, no scales, no
staff and a legal liability for the quality of what they hand over.

It is a state-capacity failure being solved by transferring it to the level of
government with the least capacity of all — and, not coincidentally, the level
run mostly by the opposition.

[? if reparations_stance = "Named as domestic instrument": Having spent
September saying the reparations note was a distraction from the energy
crisis, we now have the crisis in a photograph.?]
[? if nik_referral_channel = 1: The audit chamber can be asked to establish who
decided not to replace the banned volume, and when.?]
[? if local_network >= 30: Our own councillors and local organisers are in the
communes that have to run this, which means we hear about it before the
ministry does.?]

- @coal22_deliver
- @coal22_audit
